            <td height="24"> 
              <div align="center"><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="5" align="center"><b>Ehud 
                Barak's bigoted remarks go unnoticed</b></font></div>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr align="left" valign="top"> 
            <td height="2"> 
              <hr height="1">
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr align="left" valign="top"> 
            <td height="1849"> 
              <p> <font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><b>PMWATCH</b> 
                -- June 3, 2002 -- In <a href="#target1">the June 13 issue of 
                the New York Review of Books</a>, former Prime Minister Ehud Barak 
                stated in an interview with Israeli historian Benny Morris that 
                the Palestinians are the product of a culture "in which to tell 
                a lie ... creates no dissonance. They don't suffer from the problem 
                of telling lies that exists in Judaeo-Christian culture. Truth 
                is seen as an irrelevant category. There is only that which serves 
                your purpose and that which doesn't." </font>
              </p><p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"> Ehud Barak, 
                as anyone who has followed the editorials of the New York Times, 
                the Washington Post, the Los Angeles Times, and down the line, 
                has remained an icon of "the best chance" that the Palestinians 
                could have had, if they had taken Barak's "generous offers". Editorial 
                after editorial has bitterly condemned, excoriated, and villified 
                to the point of demonization, Yasser Arafat for spurning the civilized, 
                peace-loving Barak and paving the way to the more blunt, and less 
                sellable Ariel Sharon. </font>
              </p><p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"> And yet, 
                this very paragon of dovish virtue, it turns out, is a crass racist 
                who believes that Arabs are inherently morally inferior. </font>
              </p><p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"> The question 
                now is this: will the media denounce Ehud Barak's racist comments 
                or will they continue to treat him as the darling of the "reasonable 
                camp"? Given that he has now revealed that he is no better than 
                any of the racist goons that ruled South Africa during the days 
                of Apartheid, will they question his motives while at Camp David 
                (and will they look more closely at how he reacted to the Intifada 
                -- live ammunition against stone throwers from day one), or will 
                they keep on laying the whole blame on Araft's shoulders? (See 
                <a href="#tag2">op-ed by Yoav Peled</a> in which Barak's true 
                intentions are discussed.)</font>
              </p><p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"> Please let 
                the media know that Ehud Barak is nothing less than a bigot and 
                a racist, and please impress upon them the need to denounce his 
                comments and to rethink the usual story that presents the Israelis 
                as having treated the Palestinians as full, equal partners during 
                the negotiations. </font>
              </p><p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"> For more 
                on well documented racism from Israelis against Palestinians and 
                Arabs, see: </font>
              </p><ul>
                <li><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"> Desmond 
                  Tutu's comments on Israeli Apartheid -- <a href="/sites/pmwatch.org/pmw/cast/tutu.asp">http://www.pmwatch.org/pmw/cast/tutu.asp</a> 
                  </font></li>
                <li><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif">Famous 
                  quotes from Israeli leaders -- <a href="/sites/pmwatch.org/pmw/snakebite/famousquotes.html">http://www.pmwatch.org/pmw/snakebite/FamousQuotes.html</a> 
                  </font></li>
                <li><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif">Haaretz 
                  articles on discrimination against Israeli Arabs -- <a href="/sites/pmwatch.org/pmw/snakebite/famousquotes.html">http://www.pmwatch.org/pmw/snakebite/DISCRIM_Arab_Isr.html</a> 
                  </font></li>
              </ul>
              <p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"> For articles 
                on Ehud Barak's "concessions", see: <a href="/sites/pmwatch.org/pmw/snakebite/barakoffers.html">http://www.pmwatch.org/pmw/snakebite/barakoffers.html</a> 
                </font>
              </p><p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><a name="target1"></a>Links 
                to NYRB articles: </font>
              </p><ul>
                <li><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"> Camp David 
                  and After: An Exchange (1. An Interview with Ehud Barak) -- 
                  <a href="http://www.nybooks.com/articles/15501" class="dead" aria-disabled="true">http://www.nybooks.com/articles/15501</a> 
                  </font></li>
                <li><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"> A Reply 
                  to Ehud Barak -- Robert Malley and Hussein Agha -- <a href="http://www.nybooks.com/articles/15502" class="dead" aria-disabled="true">http://www.nybooks.com/articles/15502</a> 
                  </font></li>
                <li><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"> Camp David: 
                  The Tragedy of Errors -- Robert Malley and Hussein Agha -- <a href="http://www.nybooks.com/articles/14380" class="dead" aria-disabled="true">http://www.nybooks.com/articles/14380</a> 
                  </font></li>
                <li><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"> Camp David: 
                  An Exchange -- Dennis Ross, Gidi Grinstein, Reply by Hussein 
                  Agha, Robert Malley -- <a href="http://www.nybooks.com/articles/14529" class="dead" aria-disabled="true">http://www.nybooks.com/articles/14529</a> 
                  </font></li>
              </ul>
              <p> 
